11307	{"artifactId":"LLD-792f9324fc43d95c-s1","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-792f9324fc43d95c-s1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:controller","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-02T19:45:39.574Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"pass","summary":"review: pass","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"pass","findings":[{"claimId":"n1","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"NEW IN THIS AMENDMENT: readEpicIdentity's return type is EpicIdentity = {epicSlug, createdAt, tracker?} — identity fields only, no body and no kind — so clause (ii) genuinely cannot reuse it and must read the definition artifact itself.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim. resolve.ts:111 is `interface EpicIdentity {` and :112 is `readonly epicSlug:  string;`, with the grep for the interface matching exactly once. The type carries identity fields only — no body, no kind — so the amendment's statement that clause (ii) cannot reuse readEpicIdentity is factually correct rather than a stylistic preference.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n2","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"NEW IN THIS AMENDMENT: ArtifactShape's body is typed `{ readonly tasks?: readonly PlanTask[] }` with NO `stories` field, so the body type must gain `stories` for clause (ii) to read a DEF's declared stories.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim and this is the sharpest of the new claims. resolve.ts:98 is `interface ArtifactShape {` and :106 is exactly `readonly body?: { readonly tasks?: readonly PlanTask[] };` — matched once. So `stories` genuinely is absent from the body type and must be added for clause (ii)'s DEF branch to compile.","action":"None — the amendment already records this as a mechanical consequence rather than a design choice.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n3","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"NEW IN THIS AMENDMENT: the claimed read ORDER already exists — readEpicIdentity iterates [`DEF-<hash>.json`, `ISSUE-<hash>.json`] and returns on the first usable one, so DEF-first is inherited rather than newly decided, and no precedence rule has to be invented for a dual-anchored hash.","evidence":"CONFIRMED, and it settles the precedence question I had wrongly raised as open. resolve.ts:167 is readEpicIdentity's declaration and :168 is `for (const name of [`DEF-${epicHash}.json`, `ISSUE-${epicHash}.json`]) {`. The DEF-then-ISSUE order is inherited from existing behaviour, so no new rule is invented for a dual-anchored hash.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n4","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"NEW IN THIS AMENDMENT: readArtifact returns null on an unreadable or malformed file rather than throwing, which is what makes the DEF-then-ISSUE order first-READABLE-wins and matches the per-clause degradation the error paths specify.","evidence":"CONFIRMED by the reads: resolve.ts:121 is `function readArtifact(path: string): ArtifactShape | null {` and :125 is `return null;` inside its catch. So the order really is first-READABLE-wins and a corrupt DEF falls through to the ISSUE, matching §4's per-clause degradation. Noting one probe flaw of my own making: my second grep pattern embedded `\\n` and matched 0, because ripgrep is line-based — the pattern was wrong, not the claim.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n5","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"NEW IN THIS AMENDMENT, and the riskiest number in it: 59 of 59 standalone story-scoped artifacts on disk are ordinal 1 — 55 spelled S001 and 4 spelled s1. This corroborates the one-story rule and is also why the comparison must be by ordinal rather than by the literal string S001.","evidence":"NOT VERIFIED BY THIS PROBE, and I am recording that rather than letting the number pass on my own authority. The greps run over the source tree, not over `.insrc/artifacts/`, so `\"standalone\": true` returned 2 and `\"storyId\": \"s1\"` returned 0 — hits in src fixtures, nothing like a census. The 59-of-59 figure came from a direct python scan of .insrc/artifacts earlier in this session (55 `S001` + 4 `s1` across LLD/PLAN/BUILD artifacts carrying meta.standalone === true). Two honest limits: no probe in this mechanism can confirm an artifact-data census, and the figure describes THIS repo only — another repo could differ.","action":"No change needed, because nothing depends on it: the amendment states explicitly that the lookup does NOT consult meta.standalone. The number is corroboration for the one-story rule, so weakening it does not touch the design. Keep it labelled as corroboration rather than as a guarantee.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"n6","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"CORRECTED IN THIS AMENDMENT (was the blocking MED): the arrangement exposing the reported defect IS already covered by an existing test — id-resolve.test.ts:459 builds an ISSUE-anchored epic plus a DEF-anchored epic on different hashes, asks a bare label, and asserts null — so that test must be INVERTED rather than duplicated. The artifact must no longer claim the arrangement is untested.","evidence":"THE CORRECTION IS IN PLACE, verified directly rather than by hit-count. The amended artifact BODY contains neither 'missing from every existing test' nor 'None appears in any existing test' (checked by loading the json and searching body). id-resolve.test.ts:459 is confirmed present as `test('resolver — a DEF-less bugfix epic still counts for multi-epic ambiguity ...` — so the arrangement IS covered and the artifact now says so. The 3 residual grep hits for the old phrasing resolve to: (a) this md's appended review-report tail, which QUOTES the before text as an audit record and must stay; (b) meta.review in the json, same reason; and (c) a STALE ORPHANED FIRST-PASS LLD still on disk at docs/standalone/lld-label-resolver-regression-issue-792f9324fc43d95c-E20261002792f9324/S001/LLD.md.","action":"Nothing to change in this artifact. BUT the orphan in (c) is a real hazard worth acting on separately: re-running design.story derived a NEW folder slug from the changed focus text, so the superseded first-pass LLD remains on disk — still asserting the false 'untested arrangement' claim, still carrying the ISSUE-is-story-level framing the user corrected, and indistinguishable to a reader from the live artifact. Both folders share one artifact id (LLD-792f9324fc43d95c-s1), so the json was overwritten while the first md was left behind. Delete or explicitly mark the superseded folder, and treat the folder-per-slug behaviour as its own ledger item.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"n7","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"UNCHANGED PREMISE the whole fix rests on: buildRef does not verify story existence — its story-scoped reads are optional-chained and conditionally spread, so a missing story yields a reference merely lacking storyRef/task rather than a refusal.","evidence":"CONFIRMED — the premise the whole fix rests on, unchanged by the amendment. resolve.ts:200 is buildRef's declaration and :226 is `storyRef = readTrackerBlock(storyArtifactPath(dir, 'LLD', epicHash, storyId))?.storyRef`, with the conditional spread `...(storyRef !== undefined ? { storyRef } : {})` also matching. A missing story yields a reference lacking those optional fields rather than a refusal, so the dummy ref is real.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n8","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"UNCHANGED PREMISE 1 depends on this: exactly THREE DEF-specific sites exist in resolve.ts — the DEF_RE definition (:75), its use in listEpicHashes (:148), and its use in resolveByIssue's third pass (:294).","evidence":"CONFIRMED. All three reads landed exactly: :75 `const DEF_RE  = /^DEF-([0-9a-f]{16})\\.json$/;`, :148 `const m = DEF_RE.exec(f);` (listEpicHashes) and :294 `const m = DEF_RE.exec(f);` (resolveByIssue's third pass). The repo-wide count of 12 includes other modules and artifacts; within resolve.ts it is exactly these three, so the closed union premise 1 rests on holds.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n9","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The two corrected citations now resolve to the lines they quote: the ambiguity gate at resolve.ts:340 and readEpicIdentity's declaration at resolve.ts:167.","evidence":"BOTH PRIOR CITATION FIXES VERIFIED. resolve.ts:340 now reads `if (hashes.length !== 1) return null;   // ambiguous — needs an issue# or hierId` — the exact line c5 quotes — and :167 is readEpicIdentity's declaration rather than its doc-comment tail. The two auto-applied edits from the first review landed correctly.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n10","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"storyArtifactPath supplies clause (i) unchanged: it stats the exact name first, then scans for a sibling denoting the same ORDINAL via storyIdToOrdinal, so the three story-id spellings unify without new code.","evidence":"CONFIRMED. storyArtifactPath at resolve.ts:183 with the ordinal-scan fallback `storyIdToOrdinal(m[2]!) === want` matching, so clause (i) reuses existing machinery and the three story-id spellings unify without new code — which is also why the 4 `s1` spellings among standalone artifacts are harmless.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n11","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"resolveWorkflowRef still has exactly two non-test consumers outside resolve.ts — render.ts:54 (already passing an explicit scope) and bugfix/mount.ts:116 (injecting by reference) — so the amendment introduces no new caller obligation.","evidence":"CONFIRMED. render.ts:54 is `const ref = resolveWorkflowRef(repoPath, target, { epicHash });` and bugfix/mount.ts:116 is `resolveRef:      resolveWorkflowRef,`. The repo-wide 50-hit count was truncated and is dominated by tests plus resolve.ts itself, consistent with two non-test consumers outside the module. The amendment adds no caller obligation.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"n12","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A DEF declares its stories in body.stories with an `id` per entry — the fact clause (ii)'s DEF branch reads. Confirmed by two consumers outside this module.","evidence":"CONFIRMED verbatim at both sites: orchestrator.ts:1750 `const epicStoryIds = epic.body.stories.map(s => s.id);` and chain.ts:161 `const stories = define.artifact.body.stories;`. A DEF does declare its stories with an `id`, which is exactly what clause (ii)'s DEF branch reads — and these same two sites remain where the DEF/ISSUE equivalence does not hold outside resolve.ts, as §9 records.","action":"None.","fixability":"auto"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":0,"low":12},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-02T19:45:39.574Z","model":"client"},"resolutions":{"cl12":{"findingId":"cl12","status":"resolved","note":"Applied: the exposing arrangement IS covered by id-resolve.test.ts:459 but asserts the regressed expectation, so the label defect shipped mis-asserted rather than untested. That test must be INVERTED, not duplicated; the untested mirror direction (label in the DEF epic, ISSUE unrelated) is the genuine addition. The nonexistent-story half of the original claim stands.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-02T19:30:58.385Z"}}}}}}
